REGISTER WIDTH

128 bits (to be able to store IPv6).


REGISTER SETS

 - void
 - local
 - arguments
 - parameters

Each set has at most 64 registers, indexed from 0.


REGISTER ACCESS FORMAT

 7  6|5          0|
+----+------------+
| rs |    index   |
+----+------------+


GENERAL INSTRUCTION FORMAT

 63    48|47                     0
+--------+------------------------+
| opcode |        workload        |
+--------+------------------------+


T-FORMAT

Three-way (triple) register access.

 63    48|47  24|23 16|15  8|7   0
+--------+------+-----+-----+-----+
| opcode |      | out | lhs | rhs |
+--------+------+-----+-----+-----+

Bits 47...24 are reserved and have unspecified value.


D-FORMAT

Two-way (double) register access.

 63    48|47        16|15  8|7   0
+--------+------------+-----+-----+
| opcode |            | dst | src |
+--------+------------+-----+-----+

Bits 47...16 are reserved and have unspecified value.


S-FORMAT

One-way (single) register access.

 63    48|47               8|7   0
+--------+------------------+-----+
| opcode |                  | rgr |
+--------+------------------+-----+

Bits 47...8 are reserved and have unspecified value.


M-FORMAT

Two-way register access with 16-bit memory offset and 8-bit specifier.
"M" name is used because the format is used for loads and stores, which interact
with -M-emory.

 63    48|47 40|39 24|23 16|15  8|7   0
+--------+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+
| opcode |     | off | spc | dst | src |
+--------+-----+-----+-----+-----+-----+

Bits 47...40 are reserved and have unspecified value.


I-FORMAT (old F-FORMAT)

One-way register access with 32-bit wide immediate value.
"I" name is used because of the -i-mmediate value.

 63    48|47 40|39              8|7   0
+--------+-----+-----------------+-----+
| opcode |     |    immediate    | rgr |
+--------+-----+-----------------+-----+

Bits 47...40 are reserved and have unspecified value.


E-FORMAT

One-way register access with extended 36-bit wide immediate value.
"E" name is used because of the -e-xtended immediate value.

 63    48|47 44|43              8|7   0
+--------+-----+-----------------+-----+
| opcode |     |    immediate    | rgr |
+--------+-----+-----------------+-----+

Bits 47...44 are reserved and have unspecified value.


U-FORMAT (old R-FORMAT)

Two-way register access with 32-bit immediate value.
"U" name is used because of its -u-sefulness.

 63    48|47             16|15  8|7   0
+--------+-----------------+-----+-----+
| opcode |    immediate    | dst | src |
+--------+-----------------+-----+-----+


OPCODE ENCODING

  15 |14 12| 11 |10    0|
+----+-----+----+-------+
| gr | fmt | us | instr |
+----+-----+----+-------+

gr: greedy flag
fmt: format
us: unsigned flag
instr: instruction

The "us" is used to denote instructions constructing unsigned values (as the VM
must have some way of recognising that it should create an unsigned instead of
signed value), but nothing prevents it from being interpreted to have a
different meaning when signedness is irrelevant. Thus, full 12 bits are
available to enumerate instructions, giving the VM a limit of 4096 possible
instructions in each 7 formats (for a total 28'672 possible instructions).
